<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free City of Frankfurt</h1><p class="st-meta">1816 CE – 1866 CE · capital Frankfurt am Main</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing city-republic on the Main that hosted the German Federal Diet and the 1848 Frankfurt Parliament.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, a free imperial city for centuries and the place of imperial elections and coronations, had been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Dalberg</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored it in 1815 as one of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a>, and a constitution was adopted in 1816.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was governed by a senate and legislative assembly dominated by its wealthy Lutheran citizens. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-convention-german-confederation">Federal Diet</a> and a major banking centre, home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rothschild-family">Rothschild</a> bank. In 1848-49 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>, the first freely elected German national assembly, met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-pauls-church-frankfurt">St Paul's Church</a> and drafted a constitution for a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1866. Prussian troops occupied it, imposed a heavy indemnity, and the city was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hesse-nassau">Province of Hesse-Nassau</a>; its mayor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-fellner">Karl Fellner</a>, took his own life.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-city-of-frankfurt.jpg" alt="Free City of Frankfurt" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_City_of_Frankfurt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1816 CE – 1866 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Frankfurt am Main</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freie Stadt Frankfurt</dd></dl></aside></div>
